chler"}],"publicationYear":2006,"pageCount":496,"thumbnailUrl":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/530733-L.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"reference","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0.079,"traits":[{"id":"essayistic","score":1,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"essay\"","catalog text contains \"essays\"","catalog text contains \"criticism\"","literature/criticism subject or topic"]},{"id":"reference","score":0.44,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"atlas\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"renownProfile":{"knowsBook":true,"publicFame":35,"criticalRenown":52,"controversy":12,"llmAffinity":78,"affinityResidual":2.19,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.86,"criticalRenown":0.8,"controversy":0.77,"llmAffinity":0.92},"tags":{"craft":"prose-style","evidence":"synthesis-of-scholarship","stance":"revisionism"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"summary":"Stories are the surest way to know a place, and at a time when the fabric of the country seems daily more uncertain, Noah Richler looks to our authors for evidence of the true nature of Canada. He argues why fiction matters and seeks to discover — in the extra-ordinary diversity of communities these writers represent — what stories, if any, bind us as a nation. Over two years, Richler has criss-crossed the country and interviewed close to one hundred authors — a who’s who of Canadian literature, including Wayne Johnston, Michael Crummey, Alistair MacLeod, Gil Courtemanche, Jane Urquhart, Joseph Boyden, Miriam Toews, Yann Martel, Fred Stenson, Douglas Coupland, and Rohinton Mistry — about the places and ideas that are most meaningful to their work. The result is a journey through the reality of Canada and its imagination at a critical point in the country’s evolution. Within thematic chapters he exposes our “Myths of Disappointment” and considers the stories of our native peoples, the rise of the city, and how our history as a colony shapes our society and politics even today. This Is My Country, What's Yours? is an impassioned literary travelogue and a vivid portrayal of our society, the work of Canadian authors, and the idea of writing itself. This Is My Country, What's Yours?","displaySummary":"The result is a journey through the reality of Canada and its imagination at a critical point in the country’s evolution. Within thematic chapters he exposes our “Myths of Disappointment” and considers the stories of our native peoples, the rise of the city, and how our history as a colony shapes our society and politics even today. This Is My Country, What's Yours? is an impassioned literary travelogue and a vivid portrayal of our society, the work of Canadian authors, and the idea of writing itself. This Is My Country, What's Yours?","primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","primaryTopic":"Indigenous History","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Arts & Criticism","score":20,"evidenceCount":4},{"subject":"History","score":6,"evidenceCount":2},{"subject":"Society & Culture","score":5,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"In literature","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":5,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-open-library-this-is-my-country-what-s-yours-a-literary-atlas-of-canada-noah-richler","id":"subject-evidence-book-this-is-my-country-what-s-yours-a-literary-atlas-of-canada-noah-richler-2"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Literature and society","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":5,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-open-library-this-is-my-country-what-s-yours-a-literary-atlas-of-canada-noah-richler","id":"subject-evidence-book-this-is-my-country-what-s-yours-a-literary-atlas-of-canada-noah-richler-4"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Canada in literat